  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nRecent upper-air and surface analyses depict a mid-latitude cyclone \nover western Ontario with an attendant cold front draped across the \nNorthern Plains and another surface boundary just west of the \nMississippi River. Meanwhile, the upper-level ridge that has \nengulfed much of the CONUS continues to be suppressed by the cyclone \nand various shortwave disturbances running through the mean flow. \n\nThough the upper-level ridge is on a weakening trend, it remains \ninfluential enough for the continuation of hazardous heat this \nafternoon. Dewpoints in the mid/upper 70s have held strong with most \nsites recording heat indices above 100F (and a few above 105F) \nbefore noon today. Unfortunately, dewpoints are expected to remain \nlargely unchanged if not slightly higher than current observations \nthis afternoon given light surface winds and weak mixing. As \ntemperatures increase this afternoon, expect widespread heat indices \nin the 105-110F range. A Heat Advisory is in effect areawide through \n9 PM tonight, with a section of counties in east-central Kansas and \nwest-central/central Missouri staying under a Heat Advisory until 7 \nPM Tuesday. \n\nAs the cyclone advances to the east, it will drag the cold front \nobserved over the Northern Plains through the Midwest overnight and \nthrough the early morning hours on Tuesday. Storms are anticipated \nto develop along and ahead of this front in Wisconsin and Iowa and \nperhaps as far west as Nebraska later this evening. CAMs aren't \nparticularly excited about the prospect of strong storms closer to \nhome...many now suggest that storm clusters will decay substantially \nbefore reaching the northern Missouri border, an area where the pre-\nexisting storms would have to contend with poor shear and limited \nupper-level support. Storms may be able to sustain themselves for a \nbrief period after moving south have held onto 30-40% PoPs across \nnorth-central and northeast Missouri after midnight. The sooner any \nstorms are able to reach these areas, the greater the chance of \nseeing a strong/severe storm. The primary hazard would be damaging \nwinds, but trends point to weakening storms by the time they reach \nnorthern Missouri. Additional storm development along the cold \nfront as it moves through Kansas and Missouri is not expected \ngiven a stout layer of dry air in the low/mid levels. For most, \nthe only impact from this frontal passage will be cooler \ntemperatures...though areas under the Heat Advisory through 7 PM\nTuesday (generally south of I-70) may not get as much reprieve \nfrom the heat until Wednesday. Temperatures will continue to \ngradually cool as we progress through the remainder of the work \nweek. but a rebuilding ridge looks to heat the region back up \nover the weekend. \n\nRain chances will return midweek as a series of shortwave \ndisturbances roll through the Plains, interacting with the remnants \nof Tropical Depression 2 and resulting in a setup almost\nreminiscent of a Predecessor Rainfall Event (PRE). During \nmidweek, high pressure is expected to build across the Southern \nPlains as TD 2 drifts into the western Gulf. The proximity of \nthese features will allow deep tropical moisture to wrap \nanticyclonically into the Southwest, Great Plains, and Midwest. \nOur first chance at precipitation will arrive Thursday morning, \nlikely in the form of a decaying complex of showers/storms out \nof western/central Kansas...currently, 40-50% PoPs exist in the \nwestern portions of the CWA for this reason. Greater chances \n(60%+) arrive overnight Thursday into Friday morning when the \nlow-level jet intensifies, pulling more tropical moisture into \nthe area. Forecast PWATs currently sit in the 2-2.5\" range which\nis well above the 90th percentile climatology for TOP. \nUnsurprisingly, WPC has highlighted much of the area in a Slight\nRisk for excessive rainfall on both Thursday and Friday. Though\nwe are still a bit of a ways out, it does appear we are looking\nat some degree of a heavy rain setup for the latter half of the\nweek.",
